# Rules loaded by auditctl whenever auditd starts (RULEFILE_STARTUP in
# /etc/conf.d/auditd). Each line is the arguments of one auditctl call.
#
# auditd is here to keep SELinux AVC denials, which the kernel sends on its
# own: no syscall rules are needed for them. Gentoo's default `exit,never`
# rules are dropped on purpose, as they strip the SYSCALL record that tells
# which call an AVC came from.

# Delete every rule first, so a restart does not append to the old set
-D

# Buffers to survive bursts of denials, e.g. right after a relabel
-b 8192

# On a lost event, log it to the kernel log rather than panic (the default,
# kept explicit)
-f 1

# vim:ft=conf:
